<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-tryptophan degradation VI (via tryptamine)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3181" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE Tryptamine| is an important molecule in eukaryotes. In mammals, |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE| is an endogenous neuroactive metabolite of |FRAME: TRP|, with behavioral, physiological and pharmacological effects |CITS: [8098507]|. In plants, |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE| is a merging point of primary and secondary metabolism. Tryptamine, which is derived from tryptophan by the action of |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.105|, provides the indole unit of monoterpenoid-indole and derived alkaloids, many of which are psychoactive |CITS: [8868227][9490777]|. Tryptophan decarboxylase has been cloned from |FRAME: TAX-4058| and overexpressed in |FRAME: TAX-4097|, resulting in high tryptamine levels and resistance to whitefly |CITS: [12228625]|. The pathway from |FRAME: TRP| to |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| via |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE| is widely spread in plants and fungi |CITS: [8868227]|, but little information is available about tryptamine in bacterial metabolism. Mitoma and Udenfriend |CITS: [14423022]| demonstrated that strains of |FRAME:TAX-1351| possess tryptophan decarboxylase activity, transforming tryptophan to tryptamine. However, since their enzyme preparation also had tyrosine and phenylalanine decarboxylase activity, and taking into account the very high Km that enzyme had for tryptophan (13 mM) , they were not sure whether tryptophan decarboxylation in that organism was a result of a distinct enzyme or a secondary activity of |FRAME: EC-4.1.1.28|. Perley and Stowe |CITS: [4960870]| demonstrated production of tryptamine from tryptophan in |FRAME:TAX-1396| strain KVT. The enzyme required pyridoxal phosphate as a cofactor, and had an optimum pH of 8.0. Buki et al |CITS: [4036551]| isolated and partially purified a tryptophan decarboxylase from another &lt;i&gt;Bacillus&lt;/i&gt; strain. Nakazawa et al detected an aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase in several bacterial species that was highly active against tryptophan, forming tryptamine |CITS: [4441405][Nakazawa77]|. The highest activity was detected in several |FRAME: TAX-1269| species. The decarboxylase was produced constitutively, but was repressed by high concentrations of tryptamine. It has been suggested that the soil bacterium |FRAME:TAX-192|, which lives in association with the roots of grasses and cereals, possesses a pathway for the production of IAA from tryptophan via tryptamine |CITS: [Hartmann83][11129057]|. The organism was able to convert tryptamine, which was added to the growth medium, to IAA, but no enzyme has been identified.</Description>
